.TH "ZOXIDE" "1" "2026-07-30" "" "zoxide"
.SH NAME
\fBzoxide-dedupe\fR - merge database entries that refer to the same directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zoxide dedupe [PATHGLOBS] [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The database can accumulate multiple entries that refer to the same directory
under different spellings: case variants (\fB~/Projects\fR vs
\fB~/projects\fR) and Unicode normalization variants. Each spelling accrues
rank independently, splitting the score that ranking depends on. This command
collapses each group of equivalent entries into a single entry that receives
the group's combined score: ranks are summed and the most recent access time
is kept.
.sp
Entries are first grouped by textual equivalence (Unicode case folding plus
normalization). By default, each group is then checked against the
filesystem, and only entries that the filesystem reports as the very same
directory are merged; on a case-sensitive filesystem where both spellings
exist as distinct directories, nothing is merged. Groups of one entry are
left untouched, as are entries whose directories no longer exist (see
\fB--assume-insensitive\fR).
.sp
PATHGLOBS select which entries are processed, and are matched against the
full stored path (the same matching as \fB_ZO_EXCLUDE_DIRS\fR, described in
\fBzoxide\fR(1)). Use \fB'*'\fR to process the whole database.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -i, --assume-insensitive
Do not consult the filesystem: merge all entries that are textually
equivalent. This is required to merge entries whose directories no longer
exist, but it can merge genuinely distinct directories on case-sensitive
filesystems.
.TP
.B -n, --dry-run
Show what would be merged without modifying the database.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print help information.
